WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.473
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.371 --> 00:00:22.365
AUDIENCE: [APPLAUSE]

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:31.420
DERTOUZOS: Ladies
and gentlemen, there

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:33.970
is no reason we can't talk
about the future while having

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:36.250
a little fun.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:38.950
So let me recollect
something for you.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:42.490
Last year, a few of
my colleagues and I

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:45.280
were heading to Taiwan.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.260
The flight as you
know, is very long.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:52.300
And in the airplane, I was
trying to make something work.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.090
Now that something
was a laptop--

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:58.260
brand new laptop, the latest.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.240
It was running Windows.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:05.860
And a little card on which
you download your software--

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.340
you download your addresses,
you download your calendar,

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.990
and then you take the
card in your pocket.

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:13.990
I had been at it
for three hours.

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.750
And my problem was that when
the card software was happy,

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:21.770
the operating system wasn't, and
vice versa, as you well know.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.140
So we're in the plane.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:29.110
So I turn to one of my
colleagues, and I say, Tim,

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:30.250
would you please help me?

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:32.590
Well Tim Berners-Lee
is a very gracious man.

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.630
So he said, of course.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:36.400
He said, I think I can help you.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.380
So he picks up the machine.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:41.068
An hour and a half later,
he hands it back to me--

00:01:41.068 --> 00:01:43.360
still very polite, still very
British-- says I'm sorry,

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:44.568
I can't make this thing work.

00:01:44.568 --> 00:01:46.130
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:49.120
DERTOUZOS: So I turned to my
friend and associate director

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.140
Ron Rivest.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.770
And I say, the inventor
of the web couldn't do it.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:54.580
How about you?

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:55.413
AUDIENCE: [APPLAUSE]

00:01:55.413 --> 00:01:57.610
DERTOUZOS: Inventor of RSA?

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:02.350
And Ron, with his characteristic
wisdom, looked at me and said,

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:03.000
no, Mike.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:03.500
Thanks.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:04.930
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:07.960
DERTOUZOS: And then the youngest
member of our delegation--

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:11.500
whose name I shall not mention
to protect the innocent--

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:12.370
jumped up.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:17.410
And he says to me, you guys
are a bunch of old fogies.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:18.860
Let me show you.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:21.100
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.690
DERTOUZOS: Good LCS attitude.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:25.030
Two hours later,
he hands it to me

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:27.130
with expletives
surrounding this.

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.570
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:33.520
DERTOUZOS: I went to my seat and
I did what every expert does.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.790
I tried the various
wizards and lizards

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.070
that were coming up at me.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:42.730
And I kept typing at
random things in the fields

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:45.610
until finally, three
hours later, it worked.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:47.689
Total elapsed time nine hours.

00:02:47.689 --> 00:02:49.066
AUDIENCE: Long battery life!

00:02:49.066 --> 00:02:49.984
DERTOUZOS: [LAUGHTER]

00:02:49.984 --> 00:02:51.355
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:02:51.355 --> 00:02:53.480
DERTOUZOS: Good point for
those who make batteries.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.720
Now, ladies and gentlemen,
why do I bring this up?

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:58.000
Our field is young,
so I don't bring it up

00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.260
to complain so much.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:00.920
This is to be expected.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.090
But we are at a junction.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:07.240
What I experienced
with that example

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:10.960
is typical of what we have
done for the last 40 years

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:12.590
with our field.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:16.010
We have focused on
designing for machines.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:17.090
We put little envelopes.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:19.550
We call them operating
systems, we call them browsers.

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:21.410
We call them network software.

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:25.890
And then we throw all
this stuff at the user.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:30.420
Now the image that this
evokes to me is a user--

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:35.760
you-- sitting in your car, a
car that's very early vintage.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.570
And you've got two
rings in every finger.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:41.310
And on every ring
is attached a wire.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.590
And you've got a wire
for fuel mixture.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:47.402
You've got another wire for
advance of the ignition.

00:03:47.402 --> 00:03:49.860
You've got another one for the
valve clearances, the brakes

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:50.850
and this and that.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.250
And you're sitting there
with all these wires.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:56.880
And we say to you, go
from Boston to New York.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.320
Ladies and gentlemen,
this must stop.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.116
40 years is enough.

00:04:06.116 --> 00:04:08.496
AUDIENCE: [APPLAUSE]

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.540
DERTOUZOS: This brings me
squarely into my vision

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.790
for the 21st century.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:22.270
Stated in simple human
terms, it is simply this.

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:29.280
People should be able to
do more by doing less.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.310
Now when I say
these simple words--

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:36.990
which have been characteristic
of every revolution up to now--

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.891
I mean three things.

00:04:39.891 --> 00:04:44.210
First, we should bring the
technologies of information

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:46.070
into our lives.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:48.330
Now when I read in the
papers and the magazines

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:53.490
and I hear the pundits talk
about cyberspace where we're

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:54.810
going to go-- this other space!

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:57.750
We're going to leave our
lives and go into cyberspace.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:04:59.070
We're going to put on glasses--

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:00.900
gigabyte-infected suits.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:01.768
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:05:03.867 --> 00:05:06.200
DERTOUZOS: And we're going
to live in an exciting world.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:09.560
Well, you know, when the
Industrial Revolution came,

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:11.750
we didn't go into motor space.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:12.982
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.260
DERTOUZOS: The motors
came into our lives.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:19.720
We turned them
into refrigerators.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:23.300
And we called them
refrigerators, not motors.

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.840
We turned them into cars
and called them cars.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.570
And they absorbed
themselves-- the motors did--

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.240
into the functions and tasks
that the ancient humans

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:36.340
have been doing except
now with new tools.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:39.600
So the first thing is bring
the technology into our lives.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.360
And that's exactly what
I think will happen--

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.220
and what I wish will happen--

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.330
with the Information Age.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:49.170
They will come into our
lives-- the new technologies--

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:52.720
and they will serve
our ancient needs.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:55.470
The second thing
that I mean when

00:05:55.470 --> 00:06:01.800
I say doing more by doing less
is increased human productivity

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:03.930
and increased ease of use.

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:10.620
With respect to increased
human productivity,

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:18.730
first imagine that I'm
holding a Handheld Take

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:21.874
us to Athens this weekend.

00:06:21.874 --> 00:06:26.260
Now, you'll see later a
system that does this.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:29.090
It took me three
seconds to say that.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:31.340
The computer knows
that us is two people--

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:32.890
but we like to travel
business class.

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:36.140
It knows the weekend is
Friday, Saturday, Sunday.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:38.330
It connects with ease to Saber.

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:39.777
It starts doing the exchanges.

00:06:39.777 --> 00:06:40.610
It tries for Friday.

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:41.910
It doesn't find an aisle seat.

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:42.800
We like aisle seats.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:44.763
It goes to Saturday,
finds two seats,

00:06:44.763 --> 00:06:45.930
but something else is wrong.

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:48.800
Finally books on Sunday.

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:52.400
Total elapsed time in
actual experiments for this

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.940
is minimum three minutes
maximum 13 minutes.

00:06:56.940 --> 00:07:01.620
I will take five minutes
as an acceptable average.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:06.800
Five minutes is 300 seconds.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:07.710
Nod.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:08.210
Divided--

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:09.238
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:07:09.238 --> 00:07:11.530
DERTOUZOS: You went to Harvard
for a while, I thought--

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:12.363
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:07:12.363 --> 00:07:15.770
DERTOUZOS: Divided by
the three seconds--

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:17.750
the 300 seconds--
it gives us a ratio

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:20.700
of 100 productivity leverage.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:24.240
But in business
terms, that's 10,000%.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:25.140
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:27.930
DERTOUZOS: We have
achieved a 10,000% increase

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:29.920
in human productivity.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:31.840
Now this is actual.

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.860
Now, maybe I do it once a day
and you say it doesn't count.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:37.260
But if I was the travel agent
for a large organization,

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:39.180
it would count.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:43.290
Now I don't expect these kind
of gains across the board.

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:47.890
But I do expect across the
board in the next century--

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:50.070
and I explain this
in What Will Be--

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:54.840
about 300% increase
in human productivity,

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:58.320
primarily in the office sector.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:00.900
Today, as you look
at your browsers,

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:02.700
as you look at
your email, you're

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:04.440
sitting there
exercising your eyeballs

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:09.180
and your brains just as hard
as our predecessors exercised

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:11.430
their shovels in
the Industrial Era--

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:15.060
except you're using
these for your shovels.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:16.627
And shoveling you are!

00:08:16.627 --> 00:08:20.290
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:23.080
DERTOUZOS: But it isn't
painful because the shovels

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:24.805
are stamped high-tech.

00:08:24.805 --> 00:08:28.260
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:29.350
DERTOUZOS: All right.

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:31.690
Let's take up ease of use.

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:33.760
Every time I hear the word--

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:38.200
the phrase-- ease of use from a
vendor of hardware or software,

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:40.100
I become physically violent.

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:43.039
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:45.740
DERTOUZOS: The invariable
image that pops up

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:49.940
is of their parading a
chimpanzee around dressed

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:56.362
in a green suit pretending
to be a surgeon.

00:08:56.362 --> 00:08:57.820
I mean the thing
isn't easy to use.

00:08:57.820 --> 00:09:01.090
The fact that they put
floating animals and colors

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:05.300
and ergonomics doesn't
make it easy to use.

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:07.270
What we want is something
that's truly easy

00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:09.970
to use, regardless of whether
it's using graphics or text

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:11.450
or whatever.

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:14.390
Let me take an example.

00:09:14.390 --> 00:09:17.460
Browsers and operating systems--

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:22.380
those two worlds-- one
catering to local information,

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:24.410
the computer, with
its computer system--

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:27.480
the other one catering to
long-distance information

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:28.770
across the browser--

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:33.570
have different commands--
quite different commands.

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:37.210
Now that doesn't
lead to ease of use.

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:41.230
The image I get from that
is that of an automobile--

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:43.920
again-- which, when
you're driving it

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:45.700
in your neighborhood--

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:49.160
local-- when you turn
right, it turns right.

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:51.380
But when you're driving
it on country roads, when

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:53.804
you turn the wheel right,
it applies the brakes.

00:09:53.804 --> 00:09:55.532
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:09:55.532 --> 00:09:59.960
DERTOUZOS: It sounds silly
here, but it sounds OK

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:02.770
when you highlight an icon
in the operating system

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:06.330
and take a hot link on the
browser with the same command.

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:09.830
So we've got to revise
this kind of stuff.

00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:13.160
Now, ladies and gentlemen,
this is the second thing

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:16.460
that I mean when I
say that we should be

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:19.790
able to do more by doing less.

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:25.142
The third thing that I mean
is that when we say we,

00:10:25.142 --> 00:10:28.670
we should mean the
people of the world.

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:31.790
Now we feel pretty
smug today because we--

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:34.910
the people of the
industrially wealthy world--

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:37.260
are largely connected.

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:40.580
But if you stop and look, there
are 600 million telephones

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:44.630
only, which is 10% of the 6
billion living on this earth.

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:48.350
And in term of these 600
million telephones, only 15%

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:49.910
are interconnected--

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:52.950
about 80, 90 million people.

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:53.970
Multiply the two--

00:10:57.100 --> 00:11:00.880
1.5% to 2% percent of
the world's population,

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:03.620
ladies and gentlemen,
is interconnected.

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:04.630
That's all.

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:08.500
98% percent is disconnected.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:12.460
We cannot hear the millions
of voices from the Indian

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:16.570
subcontinent, from Africa,
and from China-- although,

00:11:16.570 --> 00:11:19.510
increasingly, they're
becoming connected there.

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:22.000
All we're getting is
the television feeds

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:23.960
and the government feeds.

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:26.530
And these people do not
have the opportunity

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:28.870
to sell their services
or their products

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:33.550
yet on the world's markets
through the new medium.

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:37.190
Now I think it's very
important to bring

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:43.970
these people in because,
left to its own devices,

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:47.240
the information revolution
will increase the gap

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:49.160
between rich and poor people.

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:53.060
The proof is
excruciatingly simple.

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:54.710
The rich get more computers.

00:11:54.710 --> 00:11:56.070
They become more productive.

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:59.300
They get richer, The
poor have no computers,

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:00.560
stay where they are--

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:03.462
formula for exponential
gap increase.

00:12:03.462 --> 00:12:05.420
Now everybody says, we
can help them to get out

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:07.160
of the problems, et cetera.

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:08.690
Yes, but we've got to help them.

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:11.370
Alone, they cannot get the
machines, the communications,

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:14.550
the training, and the
skills to do that.

00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:20.090
It's important that we do so
not only for compassion's sake

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:24.470
and for altruism, but also
for self-preservation,

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:27.440
because history teaches
that when gaps like this

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:32.580
are allowed to grow,
they result in bloodshed.

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:36.350
So, ladies and gentlemen,
there's so much potential

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:39.200
if we do help the poor--
not only poor countries,

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:42.500
but poor people within
the rich countries.

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:45.230
The kinds of
services and products

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:50.400
they could sell, the way
they could rise, and--

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:54.090
it's not always helping
from the rich to the poor.

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:56.210
Imagine a Sri Lankan doctor.

00:12:56.210 --> 00:13:01.890
A Sri Lankan doctor makes
$20 a day and speaks English.

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:07.320
Imagine that doctor connected
through the world's networks,

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:11.130
administering health care
to homeless people in Boston

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:13.210
could go to a kiosk
where there's a nurse,

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:15.360
there's a blood cuff
meter, a monitor.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:18.630
They can talk to the doctor.

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:20.630
Maybe it's not the best
health care in the world

00:13:20.630 --> 00:13:22.760
because the doctor isn't there.

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:25.460
But it's a lot better
than no health care.

00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:29.785
And it might cost
$2 or $3 per person.

00:13:29.785 --> 00:13:32.160
At the Lab for Computer Science,
a team of undergraduates

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:35.400
is building a virtual
Compassion Corps

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:37.368
that I discuss in What Will Be.

00:13:37.368 --> 00:13:38.910
This is nothing more
and nothing less

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:43.320
than a gigantic want
ad website where

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:46.650
people who wish to give
human help and people who

00:13:46.650 --> 00:13:49.800
wish to receive human help
find each other according

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:51.540
to the categories of help.

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:54.160
And they simply match
with each other and help.

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:57.300
First time in our history,
we could do this worldwide.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:06.300
So-- to make it short--

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:12.360
the vision is to let people
do more by doing less.

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:16.030
And by this I mean three things.

00:14:16.030 --> 00:14:19.450
First, bring the
technology into our lives.

00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:23.650
Second, improve human
productivity and ease of use.

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:27.930
And third, include in this
vision all people of the world.

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:31.600
Good enough for
top level vision.

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:34.080
But now, over what
computing model

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:36.540
do we see this taking place?

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:39.540
Well, for the last
20 years, you've

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:43.140
heard me talking about the
information marketplace.

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:46.830
And I'm not going to change
my tune because we haven't yet

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:48.550
realized it.

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:53.170
So the model I am proposing
is the same model--

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:57.850
the information marketplace,
which I define as follows.

00:14:57.850 --> 00:15:00.730
People and their computers--

00:15:00.730 --> 00:15:03.760
about half a billion to a
billion in the next decade--

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:07.540
plus appliances, gadgets,
embedded computers

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:13.400
and the like that are unmanned
interconnected together,

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:15.260
doing three things--

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:22.850
buying, selling, and freely
exchanging two things--

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:27.270
information and
information services.

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:29.670
Now with respect to
buying and selling--

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:34.950
currently we're at $50 billion
per year on the Internet.

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:40.840
My estimate is $4 trillion
in the next 30 years or so.

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:42.880
Doing this by
going predominantly

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:46.900
down that half of the
industrial economy which

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:48.610
is called the office sector--

00:15:48.610 --> 00:15:50.740
previously white collar work.

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:55.280
Half of our economy
is office sector.

00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:58.210
And that is where the
bulk of it will come.

00:15:58.210 --> 00:16:02.260
Now a big part of that is going
to be a new kind of activity

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:06.070
we haven't seen yet, which I
like to call office information

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:07.590
work.

00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:10.180
See, we deal with
information as a noun.

00:16:10.180 --> 00:16:15.880
Information as a noun is
text, sounds, images, videos.

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:19.600
Information is a verb
is what a doctor does.

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:22.660
It's what 1,000 accountants
do from Beijing, China,

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:25.420
when they sell their services
to General Motors in Detroit

00:16:25.420 --> 00:16:26.740
for $2 an hour--

00:16:26.740 --> 00:16:29.140
it is the processing, the
management, the handling

00:16:29.140 --> 00:16:30.685
of information, the service.

00:16:33.590 --> 00:16:36.560
That is going to be, I
believe, a very large part

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:38.510
of the information marketplace.

00:16:38.510 --> 00:16:41.390
You add to that the
non-dollar component--

00:16:41.390 --> 00:16:45.080
the family message links,
the political discussions,

00:16:45.080 --> 00:16:49.350
the publishing, the
administration of health freely

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:52.910
where it happens, do-gooder
activities, chat groups,

00:16:52.910 --> 00:16:56.900
neighborhoods-- all that
non-dollar huge component which

00:16:56.900 --> 00:16:58.500
cannot be measured.

00:16:58.500 --> 00:17:00.450
We cannot put a $4
trillion stamp on it.

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:02.580
But it's just as important.

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:07.520
And you have the picture of
this information marketplace.

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:08.839
We're not yet there.

00:17:08.839 --> 00:17:11.569
There's no information work
on the current marketplace.

00:17:11.569 --> 00:17:13.150
There is no automation.

00:17:13.150 --> 00:17:14.900
There's no capability
to do collaboration.

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:19.280
All we're doing is we have
voyeurs exhibitionists--

00:17:19.280 --> 00:17:21.980
[LAUGHTER] voyeurs
arousing, exhibitionists

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:23.480
are saying look at
what I'm selling.

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:24.200
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:26.450
DERTOUZOS: And a few little
transactions between them.

00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:30.810
So we have a little bit of
ways to go before we get there.

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:31.700
Right.

00:17:31.700 --> 00:17:37.520
So, let this be the vision
of doing more by doing less.

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:41.840
And let this be the
model over which

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:46.190
we will do more by doing less
in the information marketplace.

00:17:46.190 --> 00:17:51.230
Now that's still pretty general.

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:52.910
So how might we do that?

00:17:57.020 --> 00:18:01.350
What I would like to do next
is to announce a major research

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:05.880
project that we
are launching today

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:08.730
at the MIT Laboratory
for Computer Science.

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:12.870
We call it Oxygen. It's
a research project.

00:18:12.870 --> 00:18:15.420
It involves a new--

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:19.230
radically new--
system for tomorrow

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:23.400
that contains a lot of this
stuff that I talked about.

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:28.920
I'm very happy to say that
oxygen went as a proposal $38.5

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:31.320
million proposal to DARPA.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:34.770
And I'm very pleased to say
that DARPA awarded it to us.

00:18:34.770 --> 00:18:37.690
But they haven't
awarded the money yet.

00:18:37.690 --> 00:18:39.735
So we're negotiating that.

00:18:39.735 --> 00:18:41.360
While I'm at it, let
me say-- you know,

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:43.230
we always say bad
things about government.

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:45.008
Let me say that in
the last 25 years,

00:18:45.008 --> 00:18:46.050
I've led this laboratory.

00:18:46.050 --> 00:18:48.690
And the 10 previous
years, DARPA has

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:52.020
been with us and with
other major universities.

00:18:52.020 --> 00:18:55.320
And they've carved computer
science and technology

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:56.850
on the map.

00:18:56.850 --> 00:19:00.300
And let me say that the current
efforts of the administration

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:02.400
to increase the funding
in computer science

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:04.950
and the various activities
that have happened there

00:19:04.950 --> 00:19:06.180
are also laudable.

00:19:06.180 --> 00:19:08.605
So we shouldn't always
complain about them.

00:19:08.605 --> 00:19:09.480
They've been with us.

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:14.190
And this birthday is
largely due to them.

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:15.840
All right.

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:21.090
Oxygen. Now let me make
one more qualification

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:24.040
before I describe it to you.

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:27.070
You know, we're
not a corporation

00:19:27.070 --> 00:19:31.040
with a smooth presentation
about a new system.

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:33.100
We have a framework.

00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:35.630
Now, I'm not going
to be surprised--

00:19:35.630 --> 00:19:36.530
because I'm certain--

00:19:36.530 --> 00:19:38.030
that when we're
done, the thing will

00:19:38.030 --> 00:19:41.278
be totally different from
what I described to you today.

00:19:41.278 --> 00:19:43.070
As you hear the talks
of my colleagues, who

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:46.800
will speak about various pieces
of this, keep that in mind.

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:48.800
And as you hear the
talks of the others, who

00:19:48.800 --> 00:19:51.590
will talk about other
things while pretending

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:54.680
to talk about the
pieces, keep in mind

00:19:54.680 --> 00:19:58.910
that this laboratory does a
lot of independent research.

00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:02.240
Keep in mind, too, that, as
we do all this systems work,

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:05.090
our eyeballs are
focused on trying

00:20:05.090 --> 00:20:08.120
to understand the
fundamentals and the theories

00:20:08.120 --> 00:20:11.780
behind this brave
new field of ours.

00:20:11.780 --> 00:20:14.610
With that as background,
what is Oxygen?

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:21.050
Oxygen is 8 technologies,
which I will now enumerate

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:23.100
and describe very briefly.

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:29.110
Number one, the handy 21--

00:20:29.110 --> 00:20:32.170
for 21 is for 21st century.

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:35.400
You see it has a
little display right

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:42.530
there is a little display,
which you can see.

00:20:42.530 --> 00:20:44.200
If you're nearsighted, it helps.

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:45.610
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:20:45.610 --> 00:20:49.180
DERTOUZOS: It's like
a cellular phone,

00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:53.620
except that it has in it a
small screen, as you've seen.

00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:56.290
It has a very small analog part.

00:20:56.290 --> 00:20:57.880
The rest is all
digital, which means

00:20:57.880 --> 00:20:59.800
it can change like a chameleon.

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:02.270
It's got an infrared detector.

00:21:02.270 --> 00:21:05.230
And it's got, of course, a
few other odds and ends--

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:08.650
which permit it,
with downloading

00:21:08.650 --> 00:21:13.120
to alter its function from
a two-way radio talking

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:16.690
to other such handhelds,
to a network node talking

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:21.370
to the campus network, to a
cell phone which sniffs the air

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:24.760
and decides I'm in France, so
I'm going to switch to GSM,

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:27.160
or it says I'm in
America, I'm using CDMA.

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:29.060
And on and on and on.

00:21:29.060 --> 00:21:31.060
We have done this early
prototype in connection

00:21:31.060 --> 00:21:35.110
with another DARPA contract
that Victor Zue and McCluskey

00:21:35.110 --> 00:21:37.810
and Clark are involved in.

00:21:37.810 --> 00:21:40.520
And maybe we'll
hear more about it.

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:44.260
But that is the first
technology of Oxygen.

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:49.570
What we're trying to do with
that is make sure that you have

00:21:49.570 --> 00:21:53.940
a thing that is person-centric
wherever you go--

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:55.950
is with you.

00:21:55.950 --> 00:21:58.920
In the symmetric form, the
second technology of Oxygen

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:04.740
is the enviro 21, which
is environment-centric.

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:06.660
It sticks to the wall--

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:09.810
actually, it's big, so it may be
in the wall or under the desk.

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:11.970
It's in the trunk of your car.

00:22:11.970 --> 00:22:14.090
It's in your home.

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:19.180
It is in every respect like
the handy 21, functionally,

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:21.490
but it is much bigger.

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:25.160
So it bears a relationship of
a power socket to a battery,

00:22:25.160 --> 00:22:27.480
if you wish.

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:31.020
Also, the enviro
21 has tentacles,

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:34.790
which are connected to
sensors and actuators

00:22:34.790 --> 00:22:37.760
in the famous appliances
we're talking about.

00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:39.410
And it can control them.

00:22:39.410 --> 00:22:43.400
And also, the handy
has a little infrared

00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:45.780
in it in our current vision.

00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:50.660
And if you want to bring
this door into your world

00:22:50.660 --> 00:22:52.860
you put an infrared
tag on the door.

00:22:52.860 --> 00:22:54.680
And when you point
to handy to it,

00:22:54.680 --> 00:22:57.510
the machine identity of the
door comes into your system.

00:22:57.510 --> 00:23:00.345
And then you could bring
up a model that says,

00:23:00.345 --> 00:23:01.970
these are the people
working behind it.

00:23:01.970 --> 00:23:03.450
And this is what they're
supposed to be doing.

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:05.240
And you can go down
the corridor and check

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:08.460
what's behind every
door-- at least virtually.

00:23:08.460 --> 00:23:13.290
So interesting
possibilities with Oxygen.

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:16.830
The third technology is N-21--

00:23:16.830 --> 00:23:21.300
Network 21-- which is what we
have to add to the Internet

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:26.010
to make possible the
incorporation of these highly

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:30.360
mobile nodes called people
because the internet goes

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:34.320
to fixed addresses and we want
to go to resolve according

00:23:34.320 --> 00:23:36.580
to intention.

00:23:36.580 --> 00:23:39.880
It also makes possible
networks that rise and collapse

00:23:39.880 --> 00:23:42.040
as people get together
for a meeting,

00:23:42.040 --> 00:23:44.480
makes possible networks
of unknown elements,

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:46.780
fuzzy networks with
self-adaptation--

00:23:46.780 --> 00:23:50.300
and we'll hear more
about this later on.

00:23:50.300 --> 00:23:55.420
These are the three hardware
technologies of Oxygen--

00:23:55.420 --> 00:23:58.220
with a lot of software
in them, of course.

00:23:58.220 --> 00:24:01.750
The fourth technology
may surprise you

00:24:01.750 --> 00:24:06.170
because it is an inner
hardcore technology.

00:24:06.170 --> 00:24:10.720
It is built-in
speech understanding.

00:24:10.720 --> 00:24:14.900
We really want our
machines to be easy to use.

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:16.900
We have to make them
interact with us naturally.

00:24:16.900 --> 00:24:21.660
None of us is born with keyboard
sockets or mouse sockets

00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:23.310
on our belly.

00:24:23.310 --> 00:24:27.030
We're born with mouths and ears
and that's what we should use.

00:24:27.030 --> 00:24:31.080
Dr. Zue's technologies are
fantastic at the narrow domain

00:24:31.080 --> 00:24:33.600
level, as you heard
from Bill Gates.

00:24:33.600 --> 00:24:35.700
What we are doing now
is stitching together

00:24:35.700 --> 00:24:38.790
narrow domains to create
a broader listening

00:24:38.790 --> 00:24:42.270
domain with some
human help so that it

00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:45.630
can help us navigate through
these various narrow places

00:24:45.630 --> 00:24:47.580
and still talk to it.

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:51.320
The handy 21 has no keyboard.

00:24:54.230 --> 00:24:58.940
The fifth Oxygen technology
is individualized

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:00.680
knowledge access.

00:25:00.680 --> 00:25:02.810
By this, I mean
that people should

00:25:02.810 --> 00:25:06.530
be able to access knowledge
in ways familiar to them.

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:09.320
And we're talking about
low-hanging fruit here.

00:25:09.320 --> 00:25:11.365
We're not talking about
high-falutin concepts.

00:25:11.365 --> 00:25:12.740
You should be able
to say, get me

00:25:12.740 --> 00:25:15.260
that red book next to the
blue one or next the document

00:25:15.260 --> 00:25:17.620
that came from Paris yesterday.

00:25:17.620 --> 00:25:20.110
And then if you run
out from your own store

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:24.570
of goods and knowledge, you go
to the store of your friends.

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:28.030
And you say, assuming
they permit you,

00:25:28.030 --> 00:25:29.790
do you know the answer
to this question?

00:25:29.790 --> 00:25:31.960
Can I access this from you?

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:35.250
And if that doesn't work,
you can go out to the web

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:37.500
and get the 5,000 hits.

00:25:37.500 --> 00:25:41.240
But then in Oxygen, we
triangulate between those three

00:25:41.240 --> 00:25:44.190
to try to tailor big
hits with friends

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:46.410
and individual knowledge.

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:51.290
The sixth Oxygen
technology is automation.

00:25:51.290 --> 00:25:53.600
Now this is the
biggest workhorse

00:25:53.600 --> 00:25:55.640
of the Information Age.

00:25:55.640 --> 00:25:57.440
No revolution is
a true revolution

00:25:57.440 --> 00:26:00.890
unless the machines
can do our work.

00:26:00.890 --> 00:26:02.570
Take the shovels,
throw them away,

00:26:02.570 --> 00:26:04.160
bring in the
electronic bulldozers.

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:08.060
That's what we want
with that technology.

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:11.060
In Oxygen we offer
scripts for you

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:13.760
to automate what
you're going to do.

00:26:13.760 --> 00:26:17.330
And you can setup and
control procedures

00:26:17.330 --> 00:26:19.190
that will take place
in a given time.

00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:20.870
Or when something
arrives, you can

00:26:20.870 --> 00:26:23.450
encapsulate physical
objects, deal with them

00:26:23.450 --> 00:26:24.980
together with
informational objects,

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:27.020
and create a world
around you that

00:26:27.020 --> 00:26:32.510
caters to you by offloading
work from your head and eyes

00:26:32.510 --> 00:26:34.670
to the machines.

00:26:34.670 --> 00:26:39.350
The seventh Oxygen
technology is collaboration.

00:26:39.350 --> 00:26:41.120
And this you can
think of as a method

00:26:41.120 --> 00:26:44.390
of keeping track of
meetings, a method of keeping

00:26:44.390 --> 00:26:46.340
track of who said what--

00:26:46.340 --> 00:26:49.790
open-issues-- and
a way of zeroing

00:26:49.790 --> 00:26:54.130
in from human summaries, which
may be brief, to fragments

00:26:54.130 --> 00:26:56.620
of speech that took
place in the meeting

00:26:56.620 --> 00:26:59.970
all the way to original
work in the meeting that

00:26:59.970 --> 00:27:02.540
will remind you, if you come
back later, of what you did.

00:27:05.890 --> 00:27:10.420
The last, the eighth Oxygen
technology, is customization.

00:27:10.420 --> 00:27:12.700
One of the most variable
resources in the world

00:27:12.700 --> 00:27:14.260
is human beings.

00:27:14.260 --> 00:27:18.610
And Oxygen enables us to
customize to individual needs.

00:27:18.610 --> 00:27:21.790
Besides doing this in automation
and all the other technologies

00:27:21.790 --> 00:27:25.720
I mentioned earlier, it does
this with all the software.

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:30.230
In Oxygen, there is no
shrink wrap software.

00:27:30.230 --> 00:27:32.390
Everything is downloaded.

00:27:32.390 --> 00:27:34.850
And everything is
adjusted according

00:27:34.850 --> 00:27:37.730
to whether a user asked for
it or there was an error

00:27:37.730 --> 00:27:40.430
or there was no
software made available.

00:27:40.430 --> 00:27:45.760
This then, ladies and
gentlemen, is Oxygen. Handy 21s,

00:27:45.760 --> 00:27:53.090
enviro 21s in the wall, and
Network 21, built-in speech,

00:27:53.090 --> 00:27:56.900
individualized knowledge access,
automation, collaboration,

00:27:56.900 --> 00:27:58.590
customization.

00:27:58.590 --> 00:28:01.280
Now these eight are not
operating individually.

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:03.240
They're operating together.

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:05.630
And that's where
their power lies.

00:28:05.630 --> 00:28:09.200
And now I'd like to
make a bigger claim.

00:28:09.200 --> 00:28:11.900
When we envisioned that
person with the rings

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:15.140
in his finger trying to drive
from Boston to New York,

00:28:15.140 --> 00:28:16.970
we were wishing that
we could give him

00:28:16.970 --> 00:28:20.850
a gas pedal, a steering
wheel, and brakes

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:22.920
to make it easier to drive.

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:27.990
My claim is that the five
software technologies

00:28:27.990 --> 00:28:31.170
of speech, individualized
knowledge access, automation,

00:28:31.170 --> 00:28:33.480
collaboration,
and customization,

00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:37.590
are essentially the new kids
on the block of the Information

00:28:37.590 --> 00:28:38.350
Age.

00:28:38.350 --> 00:28:40.530
That of the thousands
and millions of things

00:28:40.530 --> 00:28:44.880
we read that will be
possible, a great deal of them

00:28:44.880 --> 00:28:47.130
can be done with these five.

00:28:47.130 --> 00:28:51.300
I believe these are pretty close
to the gas pedal, the brakes,

00:28:51.300 --> 00:28:52.350
and the steering wheel--

00:28:52.350 --> 00:28:57.430
at least for the next couple
of decades, or maybe earlier.

00:28:57.430 --> 00:29:00.900
And if that is true, then
companies and individuals

00:29:00.900 --> 00:29:03.660
will do well to ask
in their companies

00:29:03.660 --> 00:29:05.850
and in their
activities, how can they

00:29:05.850 --> 00:29:11.190
maximize their own activities
under these five elements?

00:29:11.190 --> 00:29:14.050
Imagine a health
application under Oxygen.

00:29:14.050 --> 00:29:15.900
What's the individualized
knowledge access?

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:18.450
Well, it's the doctor's
records, the patient records,

00:29:18.450 --> 00:29:19.290
it's Medline--

00:29:19.290 --> 00:29:23.170
all this has to be put
together, triangulated,

00:29:23.170 --> 00:29:24.582
and help the doctors.

00:29:24.582 --> 00:29:27.040
And then you can imagine the
collaboration when doctors get

00:29:27.040 --> 00:29:29.040
together-- what they
will put on that--

00:29:29.040 --> 00:29:31.480
and the customization
for individual doctors,

00:29:31.480 --> 00:29:33.760
and the automation of
routine tasks for x-rays,

00:29:33.760 --> 00:29:35.210
et cetera, et cetera.

00:29:35.210 --> 00:29:38.650
So applications will take
their color from Oxygen

00:29:38.650 --> 00:29:44.230
as applications have in the
past if our dreams come true.

00:29:44.230 --> 00:29:49.240
Now I would like to close,
ladies and gentlemen,

00:29:49.240 --> 00:29:53.720
by observing that both
my colleagues and I have

00:29:53.720 --> 00:29:59.430
been immensely grateful
to work on this--

00:29:59.430 --> 00:30:04.178
the information revolution--
for the last many, many years.

00:30:04.178 --> 00:30:05.970
And we're very happy
and excited that we're

00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:08.610
working on it right now
at this very moment,

00:30:08.610 --> 00:30:11.990
at this very junction in change.

00:30:11.990 --> 00:30:13.790
The vision of
doing more by doing

00:30:13.790 --> 00:30:17.690
less on tomorrow's information
marketplace with Oxygen

00:30:17.690 --> 00:30:21.900
as our first step is
where I see us today.

00:30:21.900 --> 00:30:23.720
And since we're talking
about revolutions,

00:30:23.720 --> 00:30:27.560
I'd like to close with an
observation and a wish.

00:30:30.420 --> 00:30:35.150
All three revolutions
are based on things.

00:30:35.150 --> 00:30:38.270
The Agrarian Revolution
was based on the plow.

00:30:38.270 --> 00:30:41.210
The Information Revolution--
which is now happening--

00:30:41.210 --> 00:30:42.800
is based on the computer.

00:30:42.800 --> 00:30:45.590
And the one in the middle--
the Industrial Revolution--

00:30:45.590 --> 00:30:49.750
was based on the motor,
the chemicals, so forth.

00:30:49.750 --> 00:30:52.990
Well, maybe the
time has come for us

00:30:52.990 --> 00:30:56.000
to start thinking of
a fourth revolution--

00:30:56.000 --> 00:31:00.170
one that is not based
on things, but one

00:31:00.170 --> 00:31:04.520
that tries to understand
the most precious resource

00:31:04.520 --> 00:31:06.260
on our planet--

00:31:06.260 --> 00:31:07.500
ourselves.

00:31:07.500 --> 00:31:08.250
Thank you.

00:31:08.250 --> 00:31:10.650
AUDIENCE: [APPLAUSE]

00:31:13.950 --> 00:31:17.600
[MUSIC PLAYING]

